### How does TDD force contract-first thinking?

Test-Driven Development writes a failing test first, then the code to pass it, then refactors. This forces you to think about the contract before the implementation and leaves a safety net.

```java
// Step 1: Write the failing test FIRST
@Test
void loanEligibility_dtiAboveThreshold_rejected() {
    LoanApplication app = new LoanApplication(
        "SAV2001", new BigDecimal("80000"), new BigDecimal("40000"));
    assertFalse(approvalService.isEligible(app));
    // Test fails: isEligible not implemented yet
}

// Step 2: Write the code to pass
public boolean isEligible(LoanApplication app) {
    BigDecimal dti = app.getMonthlyEmi()
        .divide(app.getMonthlyIncome(), 4, RoundingMode.HALF_UP);
    return dti.compareTo(new BigDecimal("0.40")) <= 0;
}

// Step 3: Refactor (extract constant, improve naming)
private static final BigDecimal MAX_DTI = new BigDecimal("0.40");
```

### How does modular design limit blast radius?

Separate concerns into layers -- controller, service, repository -- with clear interfaces. Apply SOLID: single responsibility so each class has one job, dependency inversion so you depend on interfaces and can swap implementations and mock in tests.

```java
package com.bank.loans.service;

import com.bank.loans.repository.LoanRepository;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class LoanApprovalService {

    // Depends on interface, not implementation (DIP)
    private final LoanRepository repository;

    public LoanApprovalService(LoanRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    // Single responsibility: only approval logic
    public boolean isEligible(LoanApplication app) {
        // ... DTI check
        return true;
    }
}
```

```text
Project structure (modular):
com.bank.loans/
  controller/   LoanController.java
  service/      LoanApprovalService.java
  repository/   LoanRepository.java (interface)
  model/        LoanApplication.java
```

### How does CI/CD enforce quality automatically?

A pipeline builds, runs the full test suite, and runs static analysis on every change. Quality gates block broken or insecure code from merging.

```yaml
# .github/workflows/quality.yml
name: Quality Gate
on: [pull_request]
jobs:
  build-test-analyze:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: actions/setup-java@v4
        with:
          java-version: '21'
      - run: mvn clean verify
      - run: mvn sonar:sonar -Dsonar.qualitygate.wait=true
```

